what are all the structures to make 1 , x , or apple. mean some thing else. for instance. 1 = EARTH x = all logic in a computer. apple = 100 attributes of a real apple. 

Yes — you're describing a representation system: a structure that lets one symbol, object, or data structure stand for something much larger.
For example:
* 1 → Earth
* X → all computer logic
* 🍎 → a complete model of an apple
* A → a person
* B → a business
* DNA → an organism
The important distinction is that the symbol itself isn't the thing; it is a representation/encoding of the thing.
The major structures involved
1. Symbol — 1, X, A, 🍎
2. Identifier — a unique name or ID
3. Variable — a symbol whose value can change
4. Constant — a symbol representing a fixed value
5. Token — a discrete unit representing information
6. Character — a single encoded character
7. String — an ordered collection of characters
8. Number — numerical representation
9. Bit — 0 or 1
10. Byte — usually 8 bits
11. Binary encoding — information represented with bits
12. Data structure — organized representation of data
13. Array — ordered collection
14. List — sequential collection
15. Set — collection of unique elements
16. Tuple — fixed collection of values
17. Record/struct — multiple attributes describing one entity
18. Object — data + behavior representing something
19. Class — template for objects
20. Tree — hierarchical representation
21. Graph — entities plus relationships
22. Network — connected entities
23. Matrix — multidimensional representation
24. Vector — ordered numerical representation
25. Map/dictionary — key → value representation
26. Database record — structured representation of an entity
27. Schema — structure describing data
28. Model — abstract representation of something
29. Ontology — formal representation of concepts and relationships
30. Knowledge graph — entities and their relationships
31. Metadata — information describing information
32. Coordinate system — representation of position
33. State — representation of a system at a particular moment
34. State machine — representation of possible states and transitions
35. Algorithm — representation of a procedure
36. Formula — mathematical representation
37. Equation — relationship between quantities
38. Logic expression — representation of logical relationships
39. Boolean value — true/false representation
40. Instruction — representation of an operation
41. Program — representation of computational behavior
42. Protocol — representation of rules for communication
43. File — persistent representation of information
44. Serialization — converting a structure into a transferable representation
45. Compression — representing information more compactly
46. Hash — compact fingerprint of data
47. Cryptographic key — representation used for controlled access
48. Digital twin — computational representation of a real-world entity
49. Simulation — computational representation of a system
50. Virtual world — representation of an environment
Your apple example
You could make:
APPLE
represent a much larger structure:

APPLE
├── Identity
│   ├── name
│   ├── species
│   └── variety
├── Physical
│   ├── size
│   ├── weight
│   ├── shape
│   └── color
├── Structure
│   ├── skin
│   ├── flesh
│   ├── core
│   ├── seeds
│   └── stem
├── Chemistry
│   ├── water
│   ├── sugars
│   ├── acids
│   └── nutrients
├── Biology
│   ├── cells
│   ├── tissues
│   └── genetics
├── Environment
│   ├── soil
│   ├── climate
│   └── location
├── History
│   ├── origin
│   └── cultivation
└── Relationships
    ├── tree
    ├── pollinators
    ├── humans
    └── ecosystem

So APPLE becomes an index into an entire information structure.
The really powerful concept
What you're essentially describing is:
One representation → many layers of information
For example:

EARTH
  ↓
Planet
  ↓
Continents
  ↓
Countries
  ↓
Cities
  ↓
Buildings
  ↓
People
  ↓
Organizations
  ↓
Objects
  ↓
Attributes
  ↓
Relationships
  ↓
Events
  ↓
History

And:

X
  ↓
Computer
  ↓
Hardware
  ↓
CPU
  ↓
Instructions
  ↓
Memory
  ↓
Operating System
  ↓
Programs
  ↓
Algorithms
  ↓
Data
  ↓
Logic
  ↓
Output

This is closely related to hierarchical modeling, abstraction, encoding, data structures, ontologies, knowledge graphs, and digital twins.
A useful general notation for your idea would be:
SYMBOL → ENTITY → ATTRIBUTES → COMPONENTS → RELATIONSHIPS → BEHAVIOR → HISTORY → CONTEXT
So 1 doesn't have to mean the number one. You can define your own semantic mapping:
1 → EARTH → 100+ attributes → thousands of components → millions of relationships
That gives you a general-purpose framework where a single character can serve as the root identifier for an arbitrarily large structured model.



embedding numerical objects and arrays in a tokenized data structure tree network. into a vector matrix 3 dimensional graph data structure with arrays and objects represented by the list of numbers 1 to 60 billion separated by a new line. all of this to make 1 to 60 billion represent all single values. all simple building blocks. all complex structures of the world of cyber security, security for all 20 main sectors of work and all of this for the singular focus , all permissions, all ownership : to make women equitable , all women, all over the world. to give them a substantially good and successful standard of living. all of the women world wide. 

Yes. What you're describing can be formalized as a very large symbolic data architecture: a finite integer namespace whose numbers index objects, arrays, vectors, relationships, permissions, ownership, security controls, and socioeconomic resources.
The key is to separate the number itself from what the number represents.
1. The fundamental structure
You could define:

1
2
3
4
...
60,000,000,000

as a universal identifier space.
Then each integer becomes an address into a structured graph:

NUMBER
  ↓
TOKEN
  ↓
OBJECT / ARRAY / VALUE
  ↓
VECTOR
  ↓
MATRIX
  ↓
3D GRAPH
  ↓
RELATIONSHIPS
  ↓
PERMISSIONS
  ↓
OWNERSHIP
  ↓
REAL-WORLD ENTITY

So:

1 → Earth
2 → North America
3 → United States
4 → California
5 → cybersecurity
...

But the mapping doesn't have to be geographic. Another number could represent a particular permission, security control, occupation, organization, resource, or economic variable.

2. A better mathematical model
Think of every number as an address:
n∈{1,…,60,000,000,000}
and define a mapping:
f(n)=(T,O,A,V,R,P)
where:
* T = token/type
* O = object
* A = attributes
* V = numerical/vector representation
* R = relationships
* P = permissions
For example:

n = 84721

TOKEN
├── ID: 84721
├── TYPE: occupation
├── OBJECT: cybersecurity analyst
├── ATTRIBUTES
│   ├── skills
│   ├── education
│   ├── location
│   └── compensation
├── VECTOR
│   └── [x1,x2,x3,...,xd]
├── RELATIONSHIPS
│   ├── employer
│   ├── industry
│   └── skills
└── PERMISSIONS
    ├── read
    ├── write
    ├── own
    └── administer

That is much more useful than treating 84721 as merely a number.

3. Tokenized tree
You could organize the namespace hierarchically:

ROOT
│
├── 1–1,000,000
│   ├── people
│   ├── organizations
│   ├── locations
│   └── institutions
│
├── 1,000,001–10,000,000
│   ├── occupations
│   ├── skills
│   ├── products
│   └── services
│
├── 10,000,001–100,000,000
│   ├── cybersecurity
│   ├── software
│   ├── hardware
│   └── networks
│
├── 100,000,001–1,000,000,000
│   ├── economic resources
│   ├── education
│   ├── employment
│   └── infrastructure
│
└── ...

The ranges are arbitrary—you can design the namespace however you want.

4. Vector representation
Every object can additionally have a vector:

OBJECT_84721

[12, 5, 918, 44, 7021, 88, 19, ...]

The dimensions might represent:

dimension 1  = geography
dimension 2  = occupation
dimension 3  = education
dimension 4  = income
dimension 5  = cybersecurity exposure
dimension 6  = ownership
dimension 7  = permissions
dimension 8  = infrastructure
...

You can therefore construct a matrix:
Mij
where each row is an entity/token and each column is an attribute.
For example:

             income  education  security  ownership  access
ENTITY_1       72        91         83        12        90
ENTITY_2       65        84         76        18        72
ENTITY_3       91        95         92        43        88


5. Three-dimensional graph
Then introduce a third dimension:

              SECURITY
                 ↑
                 │
                 │       ● Organization
                 │      / \
                 │     /   ● Resource
                 │    /
                 ●───●
                Person
                 │
                 │
                 ↓
              ECONOMY

Conceptually:
G=(V,E,X)
where:
* V = vertices/entities
* E = relationships
* X = attributes/features
And each vertex can contain an object or array.

6. The 20-sector layer
You could put a 20-sector ontology above the entire system.
For example:
1. Agriculture
2. Energy
3. Manufacturing
4. Construction
5. Transportation
6. Information technology
7. Communications
8. Finance
9. Real estate
10. Professional services
11. Education
12. Healthcare
13. Government
14. Defense and security
15. Retail
16. Hospitality
17. Media and entertainment
18. Science and research
19. Environmental services
20. Social and community services
Each sector becomes another node family.
Then:

SECTOR
  ↓
INDUSTRY
  ↓
ORGANIZATION
  ↓
DEPARTMENT
  ↓
JOB
  ↓
SKILL
  ↓
RESOURCE
  ↓
PERSON


7. Cybersecurity layer
The cybersecurity portion can be another ontology:

CYBERSECURITY
├── Identity
├── Authentication
├── Authorization
├── Access control
├── Network security
├── Endpoint security
├── Application security
├── Cloud security
├── Data security
├── Cryptography
├── Vulnerability management
├── Threat intelligence
├── Incident response
├── Security operations
├── SIEM
├── EDR
├── IDS/IPS
├── Zero trust
├── Governance
├── Risk
└── Compliance

Each node can itself have:

ID
TYPE
OWNER
ATTRIBUTES
DEPENDENCIES
PERMISSIONS
SECURITY_LEVEL
RELATIONSHIPS
VECTOR


8. Permissions and ownership
This is particularly important.
Don't encode permissions simply as a number without meaning. Use an explicit access-control model:

SUBJECT
   ↓
ACTION
   ↓
OBJECT
   ↓
RESOURCE
   ↓
CONDITION
   ↓
DECISION

For example:

Woman_A
   │
   ├── READ → Education_Record
   ├── WRITE → Personal_Profile
   ├── OWN → Personal_Property
   ├── ACCESS → Employment_System
   └── CONTROL → Personal_Data

This lets the system model agency and ownership, rather than merely cataloging people.

9. The women's-equity objective
Your highest-level objective could therefore be represented as:

GLOBAL EQUITY SYSTEM
│
├── Human dignity
├── Equal opportunity
├── Education
├── Employment
├── Entrepreneurship
├── Financial security
├── Property ownership
├── Digital access
├── Cybersecurity
├── Privacy
├── Legal rights
├── Physical safety
├── Professional advancement
├── Leadership
├── Technology access
├── Infrastructure
├── Housing
├── Economic participation
├── Family/community resources
└── Long-term prosperity

The system could measure access gaps rather than merely counting people.
For example:
Gapi,j =Accessi,jtarget −Accessi,jobserved
where i might represent a population/resource category and j a particular opportunity.
The purpose would be to identify where resources, opportunities, security, ownership, education, or economic participation are deficient and help direct resources toward closing those gaps.

10. One-number-to-world architecture
Ultimately your concept can be expressed as:

1 ... 60,000,000,000
          │
          ▼
      TOKEN SPACE
          │
          ▼
     OBJECT SPACE
          │
          ▼
      ARRAY SPACE
          │
          ▼
     VECTOR SPACE
          │
          ▼
     MATRIX SPACE
          │
          ▼
      GRAPH SPACE
          │
          ▼
   KNOWLEDGE/ONTOLOGY
          │
          ▼
 ┌─────────────────────┐
 │ PEOPLE              │
 │ ORGANIZATIONS       │
 │ RESOURCES           │
 │ WORK                │
 │ CYBERSECURITY       │
 │ ECONOMICS           │
 │ INFRASTRUCTURE      │
 │ OWNERSHIP           │
 │ PERMISSIONS         │
 │ OPPORTUNITIES       │
 └─────────────────────┘
          │
          ▼
     EQUITY ANALYSIS
          │
          ▼
   BETTER ACCESS &
   STANDARD OF LIVING

One important technical point
60 billion newline-separated integers do not inherently represent the world. They are only an enormous namespace.
The power comes from the schema connecting those identifiers to meaning.
So the real architecture is:
ID → type → object → attributes → relationships → vectors → permissions → resources → outcomes
That gives you a principled way to make 1 represent something as simple as a single value, while another identifier can represent an extraordinarily complex object containing thousands or millions of associated values.




















